<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nestor Makhno</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1888">1888</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nestor Makhno was a Ukrainian anarchist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huliaipole">Huliaipole</a> who during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> led the Revolutionary Insurgent Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. His forces fought the Austro-German occupiers, the Ukrainian nationalists and the Whites, sometimes in alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, and defended a stateless zone run by peasant councils. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> crushed his movement in 1921, and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nestor-makhno.jpg" alt="Nestor Makhno" width="320" height="493" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1921._%D0%9D%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%80_%D0%9C%D0%B0%D1%85%D0%BD%D0%BE_%D0%B2_%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B3%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5_%D0%B4%D0%BB%D1%8F_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BC%D0%B5%D1%89%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BD%D1%8B%D1%85_%D0%BB%D0%B8%D1%86_%D0%B2_%D0%A0%D1%83%D0%BC%D1%8B%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Makhno was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huliaipole">Huliaipole</a>, then in the Katerynoslav Governorate, on 7 November 1888, the youngest of five children of former serfs. His father died when he was ten months old. In the 1905 revolution, at sixteen, he joined the revolutionary movement and became a member of the Union of Poor Peasants, an anarchist communist group in his home town. On 26 March 1910 a court-martial sentenced him to hang, but because of his youth the sentence was commuted to life at hard labour. In Butyrka prison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> he read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-kropotkin">Peter Kropotkin</a>'s Mutual Aid and contracted tuberculosis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Makhno returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huliaipole">Huliaipole</a> in March 1917 and was elected chairman of a Peasants' Union, which soon represented most of the peasantry of the district. In 1918 the German and Austro-Hungarian occupation forces took Huliaipole. Makhno returned secretly in July and launched an insurgency. At the Dibrivka forest he and Fedir Shchus broke an Austrian encirclement with a surprise counterattack, and the insurgents gave him the title Bat'ko, Father.</p><p>He allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> against the Whites, but his movement and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> quarrelled over its autonomy. In 1919 he denounced the otaman Nykyfor Hryhoriv for pogroms. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-peregonovka">Battle of Perehonivka</a> he routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-movement">White Army</a> and his forces held most of southern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, where they again tried to establish anarchist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>. A short-lived alliance with the Bolsheviks drove Wrangel's forces out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. In late November 1920 the Red Army attacked the Makhnovists by surprise and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huliaipole">Huliaipole</a>, and Makhno escaped with his Black Guards and retook the town a week later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In July 1921 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-frunze">Mikhail Frunze</a> demanded the definitive liquidation of the Makhnovist movement. Wounded, Makhno crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> in August 1921 with his wife Halyna and about 100 followers and was interned. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in April 1925, worked briefly in a foundry and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renault">Renault</a> factory, and wrote memoirs and articles for radical newspapers. Illness and depression marked his last years, and he quarrelled with Ida Mett and Volin over his memoirs. He died of tuberculosis on 25 July 1934, aged 45.</p><p>Makhnovist groups worked clandestinely through the 1920s, and some fought as partisans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>. Anarchists cite him as an inspiration, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huliaipole">Huliaipole</a> a statue of him stands in the main square and a museum has an exhibition devoted to him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nestor Makhno” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The offensive was halted, and Wrangel's army, pursued by the Red Army and by the cavalry and infantry of <b>Nestor Makhno</b>'s insurgents, fell back into the Crimea in November 1920 and was evacuated to Constantinople on 14 November.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-red-army-is-the-strongest">The Red Army Is the Strongest</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nestor Makhno</b>, an anarchist from Huliaipole, organised peasant partisans against the Austro-German occupiers and the Ukrainian State in 1918.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-territory">Free Territory</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huliaipole">Huliaipole</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.66,36.26&amp;year=1917" aria-label="Huliaipole on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5396 493 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nestor Makhno"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5846 763h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5846 763h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.66,36.26&amp;year=1917">What was here in 1917? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
